    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d719">

                <pb facs="0722" n="622" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg622"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/7645: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The High Commissioner at Constantinople</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Bristol</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-11-04"><hi rendition="#italic">November 4,
                        1920</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received November 7—4:34 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>557. From Admiral McCully [,Sevastopol?].</p>
                <p>“86, November 4, 10 p.m. Red offensive began October 28th, main forces consisting
                    of about 40,000 infantry and 15,000 cavalry moving from Kakhovka against Perekop
                    and Sivash but not attaining either objective. Wrangel forces had already begun
                    withdrawal from northern Tauride October 23rd, and completed it November 2nd,
                    without serious losses. Troops holding together well. All Wrangel forces now
                    concentrated in Crimea passage, strong detachments holding both Perekop and
                    Sivash which are considered secure. So far public opinion is tranquil regarding
                    retreat as inevitable under the circumstances.</p>
                <p>Wrangel intends to fully secure position in Crimea, develop liberal government on
                    lines already laid down, and await opportunity for further action. Red naval
                    forces are again active in Azov Sea and control of this sea is undecided. The
                    Azov is usually closed to navigation by ice from January until April. Naval
                    forces now become important facilities in future operations and action of
                    Wrangel <pb facs="0723" n="623" xml:id="pg623"/> naval vessels hampered on
                    account of lack of fuel. Some uneasiness about grain supply for population of
                    Crimea during the winter and no more grain will be exported. McCully. [”]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bristol</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
